  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does a stress-strain curve show?",
      "a": "It shows how a material responds as it is stretched: its stiffness, yield strength, ultimate strength and how much it stretches before breaking.",
      "asks": [
        "explain stress strain diagram"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the yield point?",
      "a": "It is the stress at which a material stops behaving elastically and starts to deform permanently. For mild steel it is about 250 megapascals.",
      "asks": [
        "what does the mild steel curve show",
        "yield strength meaning"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between yield strength and ultimate strength?",
      "a": "Yield strength is where permanent deformation begins. Ultimate tensile strength is the highest stress on the curve, reached later after strain hardening.",
      "asks": [
        "yield vs ultimate tensile strength"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does steel neck before it breaks?",
      "a": "After the ultimate strength, deformation concentrates in one spot, which thins quickly. The force needed falls, so the engineering stress drops until fracture.",
      "asks": [
        "what is necking"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is cast iron called brittle?",
      "a": "It fractures at very small strain, under one percent, without yielding or necking. It gives no warning before breaking, unlike ductile steel.",
      "asks": [
        "brittle vs ductile materials"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Mild steel typically yields at about 250 megapascals.",
    "The 0.2 percent offset proof stress is used as the yield strength for metals without a clear yield point."
  ],
